 ],
 "literal": "The salt-marsh bull, without plowing, to lie down / in the pasture land where the sweltering heat is removed by clouds / with swiftly striking thunder, so that the snake's hood is subdued / mingling with thunder, it fell pleasantly / embracing the great rain that fell, those who separated / to feel helpless, the distressing evening that came / the peacock with hollow eyes that sat on the flowering branch / as the spreading water in the wide space makes one feel lonely / cries, my friend, they are greatly foolish!",
 "translation": "The great rain has fallen pleasantly, mingling with thunder, so that the snakes' hoods are subdued by the swift thunderbolts in the pasture lands where the sweltering heat is now removed by clouds, and the salt-marsh bull lies down without plowing. Embracing this fallen rain, at this distressing evening hour that has come to make those who are separated feel helpless, the hollow-eyed peacocks sitting on the flowering branches cry out as the spreading water makes the wide space lonely. O friend, they are indeed greatly foolish!",
 "modern": "உப்பளத்து எருது உழவு செய்யாமல் படுத்து உறங்கவும், வெப்பம் தணிந்த மேகங்கள் சூழ்ந்த முல்லை நிலத்தில், வேகமாக இடிக்கும் இடியினால் பாம்புகளின் படம் சுருங்கவும், இடியுடன் கூடி நல்ல மழை பெய்துள்ளது. பெய்த பெருமழையைத் தழுவிக்கொண்டு, பிரிந்தவர்கள் செயலற்றுப் போகும்படி வந்த இந்தத் துன்பம் தரும் மாலைப் பொழுதில், பூக்கள் நிறைந்த கிளையில் அமர்ந்திருக்கும் பிளந்த கண்களை உடைய மயில்கள், பரவிய நீரால் அகன்ற இடம் தனிமை கொள்ளுமாறு கூவுகின்றன. தோழியே! அவை மிகவும் அறிவற்றவை!",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante during the evening of the rainy season, which belongs to the Mullai landscape. The hero had promised to return by this season, but he has not yet arrived. The rain has fallen, cooling the parched pasture lands, allowing the salt-marsh bull to rest, and driving the snakes into hiding with its thunder. However, this beautiful rain brings the distressing evening, which makes separated lovers feel utterly helpless. The peacocks sitting on the flowering branches cry out joyfully in the wet, lonely expanse, and the heroine calls them 'greatly foolish' because they are completely unaware of the deep pain their cries cause to her aching heart.",
